---
/**
 * ForestBand — the Pacific Northwest forest photograph that closes every page.
 *
 * REPLACES THE DRAWN TREELINE, and the reason is worth keeping because it was
 * two failed attempts. A generated conifer silhouette was tried first: version
 * one stretched, because preserveAspectRatio="none" across a 1900px page made
 * every tree 1.6 times wider than drawn and a fat conifer is a mountain.
 * Version two used "slice", which scales uniformly and then crops — and
 * because the band is far wider than the viewBox is tall, what it cropped was
 * the tapered tops. Black trapezoids. The lesson is that a horizon band wants
 * a tiled or intrinsically wide image, not a viewBox fitted to a page width,
 * and by then the owner had supplied a photograph that is better than any
 * drawing would have been.
 *
 * WHY AN <img> RATHER THAN A CSS BACKGROUND. A background-image on an element
 * near the footer downloads on page load for every reader, whether or not
 * they scroll that far. An <img loading="lazy"> does not — the browser fetches
 * it when the reader approaches it, which on a 3,000-word page is most of the
 * time never. It also lets the browser pick between the two widths below
 * rather than serving a 1536px file to a phone.
 *
 * DECORATIVE, AND IT STILL CARRIES A REAL ALT. An empty alt is the textbook
 * answer for an ornamental image and harness check 2c rejects it — the rule
 * fails any <img> whose alt does not contain text, because the failure it
 * exists to catch is a meaningful image shipped without one, and it does not
 * model the decorative case.
 *
 * The options were to loosen that rule or to write an alt. Loosening a check
 * to get past it is the thing HANDOFF.md explicitly tells the next person not
 * to do, and the rule is right far more often than it is wrong. So the wrapper
 * carries aria-hidden — which is what actually keeps this out of a screen
 * reader — and the image carries a true description underneath it. If the
 * aria-hidden is ever removed, the alt is already correct.
 *
 * The scrim at the foot of it is what makes this work rather than look pasted
 * on: the photograph fades into the footer's own black, so the page resolves
 * into the footer instead of stopping at a hard edge.
 */
---
<div class="forestband" aria-hidden="true">
  <img
    class="forestband-img"
    src="/img/scene/forest-band-1600.jpg"
    srcset="/img/scene/forest-band-900.jpg 900w, /img/scene/forest-band-1600.jpg 1600w"
    sizes="100vw"
    alt="Sunrise over a forested Cascade valley, conifers and ferns framing a mist-filled basin below a snow-capped peak"
    width="1600"
    height="683"
    loading="lazy"
    decoding="async"
  />
</div>
